#!/usr/bin/env sh
# project-atlas · the dashboard URL, in the one place it cannot scroll away
#
# The link is announced once per session — by `hooks/on-session-start.sh`, and since the adoption defect by
# `hooks/on-activity.sh` as well. Once is the right number for a printed line: repeat it after every tool
# call and it becomes noise people filter out, which costs it the one moment it needed to be read. But the
# price of printing it once is that it scrolls away, and half an hour later "where is the dashboard" is
# answered by searching scrollback for a URL whose port nobody memorised, because the port is derived from
# the repository path and is different in every checkout.
#
# The statusline is the fix, because it is not a message. It is a fact about the terminal, rendered wherever
# the user is already looking.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- why this lives in bin/ and not hooks/
#
# Every script in `hooks/` is named by `hooks/hooks.json`, where the harness expands `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}`
# for us. Nothing there is ever typed by a person. This one is the opposite: `statusLine` is a key in the
# user's own `settings.json`, so the string that names this file is written down by a human, in a file this
# plugin does not own, and it has to stay correct across upgrades.
#
# An absolute path would not. The installed plugin lives at
# `~/.claude/plugins/cache/project-atlas/atlas/0.1.64/bin/atlas-statusline` — the version is *in the path*.
# That `0.1.64` was accurate when this was written and was 0.1.65 before the change landed, which is the
# whole argument: a settings.json pointing at today's cache stops resolving at the next release, and a
# statusline command that does not resolve prints nothing, which is indistinguishable from "no server". That
# is the silent-failure shape this feature exists to remove, so the wiring must not reintroduce it.
#
# Claude Code puts an enabled plugin's `bin/` on PATH — the same mechanism that makes `atlas` a command — so
# the durable spelling is the bare name `atlas-statusline`. It survives upgrades, and disabling the plugin
# removes the segment cleanly instead of leaving a broken command behind. `--install` checks that the bare
# name actually resolves before writing it, and falls back to the absolute path while saying what that costs.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- speed
#
# This runs on session start, on every assistant message, and on every permission-mode change, debounced at
# 300ms. It reads `.atlas/serve.pid` directly and never spawns node: asking `atlas serve --status` for a
# number that is already written down would put a node startup on the critical path of the terminal's own
# redraw, and a statusline that makes the terminal feel slow is a statusline people delete. Finding the
# repository root is a shell loop over `.git` rather than `git rev-parse` for the same reason — the ascent
# costs no process at all.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- three states, and none of them lie
#
#   no repository, or one with no project-atlas.config.json      →  nothing. Zero bytes.
#   an adopted repository whose pidfile names a live pid          →  atlas · http://127.0.0.1:<port>/
#   an adopted repository, any other state                        →  atlas · dashboard down
#
# The pid is verified rather than trusted, because a killed server leaves its pidfile behind. **A statusline
# showing a dead link is the exact silent failure this feature exists to remove** — the frozen dashboard read
# as live for a whole session, now pinned to the bottom of the screen where it would be believed harder.
#
# **Adoption, not the pidfile, is what decides between silence and a line**, and getting that wrong is how
# the first version of this script reported a working dashboard as an unrelated project. A missing pidfile
# looks like "never adopted" and very often is not: the pidfile is the first casualty when servers race for
# a port, because every record this tool keeps is keyed by a pid and reaping a dead one discards the claim
# while the winner is still listening. Three of the four repositories on this developer's machine were in
# exactly that state — answering on their ports with nothing naming them.
#
# So the config file decides whether to speak at all, and after that every state that is not a live pid is
# the same fact to a reader: adopted, not currently reachable. Self-correcting, too — the next tool call
# fires `on-activity.sh`, which restarts the server and rewrites the pidfile.
#
# Signal 0 is the same bar `serve --status` holds to (`scripts/lib/serve.mjs:204`). It cannot tell a recycled
# pid from the original, and nothing cheap can; what it does rule out is the common case, which is a server
# that exited and left its claim on disk.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- composing with a statusline you already have
#
# Claude Code runs exactly one `statusLine` command, so this cannot be additive by itself and must not
# pretend to be. Three commitments make it a component rather than a takeover:
#
#   1. **One line, or nothing at all.** Every line on stdout becomes its own statusline row, so a stray
#      newline would silently steal a row from whatever else is up there.
#   2. **No ANSI.** Plain ASCII, so a wrapper can dim, colour or truncate it to match its own theme instead
#      of fighting escape codes this script chose.
#   3. **The directory can be passed as an argument.** stdin can only be drained once, so a wrapper that has
#      already read the payload for its own fields could not hand it on. `atlas-statusline "$dir"` needs no
#      stdin at all.
#
# So a combined statusline is an ordinary shell script:
#
#   #!/bin/sh
#   payload=$(cat)
#   dir=$(printf '%s' "$payload" | jq -r '.cwd')
#   atlas=$(atlas-statusline "$dir")
#   printf '%s%s\n' "$(my-existing-statusline "$dir")" "${atlas:+  |  $atlas}"
#
# `${atlas:+…}` is the point: when there is no server this expands to nothing, separator included, so an
# unadopted repository costs no empty brackets.

set -u

SELF=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)/$(basename -- "$0")

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
atlas-statusline — the live dashboard URL, for the Claude Code statusline.

  atlas-statusline [<dir>]      print one line for <dir> (default: the payload's cwd, else $PWD)
  atlas-statusline --install    add it to ~/.claude/settings.json      --project for ./.claude/settings.json
  atlas-statusline --uninstall  remove it again                        --project for ./.claude/settings.json

It reads the JSON statusline payload on stdin when there is one, and needs nothing but a directory
otherwise. Prints nothing in a repository that has not adopted project-atlas.
EOF
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- finding the repository
#
# Walks up for `.git`, which is `-e` rather than `-d` because a worktree's `.git` is a file. Bounded by
# reaching `/`, so a path outside any repository ends the loop rather than the process.
repo_root() {
  d=$1
  while [ -n "$d" ] && [ "$d" != "/" ]; do
    [ -e "$d/.git" ] && { printf '%s' "$d"; return 0; }
    d=${d%/*}
  done
  return 1
}

# The same two expressions `hooks/on-activity.sh:53,75` reads the pidfile with, deliberately identical: if
# the file's shape ever changes, one grep has to find every reader of it.
pid_of()  { sed -n 's/.*"pid"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*\([0-9]*\).*/\1/p'  "$1" 2>/dev/null; }
port_of() { sed -n 's/.*"port"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*\([0-9]*\).*/\1/p' "$1" 2>/dev/null; }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- wiring it up
#
# **Editing settings.json is a change to the user's environment, and this asks before it makes one.**
# `docs/references/autonomy.md` grants autonomy over derived state and stops at the edge of the repository;
# `~/.claude/settings.json` is neither derived nor inside it. So there is no hook that installs this, and
# nothing writes the key as a side effect of anything else. It happens when someone types the command.
#
# Two things then keep the command itself honest:
#
# - **It refuses to overwrite a statusLine it did not write.** A user's statusline is their text, and
#   silently replacing it is the same act as `publish --force` overwriting somebody's wiki page. It prints
#   what is already there and the recipe for composing with it, and exits 1.
# - **An install that did not happen is never reported as done.** Without `jq` it writes nothing and prints
#   the exact JSON to paste — the same rule the reports follow, that a step which could not run is never
#   reported as having run.
settings_file() {
  if [ "$1" = project ]; then
    r=$(repo_root "$(pwd)") || { echo "atlas-statusline: --project needs to be run inside a git repository." >&2; exit 1; }
    printf '%s' "$r/.claude/settings.json"
  else
    printf '%s' "${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/settings.json"
  fi
}

# The bare name is the durable spelling, but only if it actually resolves — see the header. Checked rather
# than assumed, because the failure of an unresolvable command is an empty statusline, which looks exactly
# like a working one with no server running.
installed_command() {
  if command -v atlas-statusline >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf 'atlas-statusline'
  else
    printf '%s' "$SELF"
  fi
}

install_it() {
  scope=$1
  file=$(settings_file "$scope") || exit 1
  cmd=$(installed_command)
  snippet="\"statusLine\": { \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"$cmd\" }"

  if ! command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "atlas-statusline: jq is not installed, so nothing was written."
    echo "Add this to $file yourself:"
    echo ""
    echo "  $snippet"
    exit 1
  fi

  if [ -e "$file" ]; then
    if ! jq -e . "$file" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      echo "atlas-statusline: $file is not valid JSON. Nothing was written — fix it first." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    existing=$(jq -r '.statusLine.command // ""' "$file")
    case $existing in
      ''|atlas-statusline|*/atlas-statusline) ;;
      *)
        echo "atlas-statusline: $file already has a statusLine, and it is not this one:"
        echo ""
        echo "  $existing"
        echo ""
        echo "That is your configuration and this will not overwrite it. To run both, point statusLine at a"
        echo "script of your own that calls each and joins the results — \`atlas-statusline --help\` shows one."
        exit 1
        ;;
    esac
  else
    mkdir -p "$(dirname -- "$file")" || exit 1
    echo '{}' > "$file" || exit 1
  fi

  tmp="$file.atlas.$$"
  # Written to a temporary file and moved into place, so an interrupted or failing jq leaves the settings
  # exactly as they were rather than truncated. This file is not ours to half-write.
  if jq --arg c "$cmd" '.statusLine = { type: "command", command: $c }' "$file" > "$tmp" 2>/dev/null; then
    mv -- "$tmp" "$file"
  else
    rm -f -- "$tmp"
    echo "atlas-statusline: could not write $file. Nothing was changed." >&2
    exit 1
  fi

  echo "atlas-statusline: added to $file"
  echo "  $snippet"
  if [ "$cmd" != atlas-statusline ]; then
    echo ""
    echo "  Written as an absolute path because 'atlas-statusline' does not resolve on this PATH. That path"
    echo "  contains the plugin version, so re-run this after a plugin update — or add the plugin's bin/ to"
    echo "  PATH and re-run it once to switch to the durable spelling."
  fi
  echo ""
  # The undo instruction is spelled the same way the installed command is. Printing `atlas-statusline
  # --uninstall` to someone for whom that name does not resolve hands them a reversal that does not run,
  # which makes "reversible" a claim rather than a fact.
  echo "Takes effect in the next session. Undo with: $cmd --uninstall$([ "$scope" = project ] && printf ' --project')"
}

uninstall_it() {
  scope=$1
  file=$(settings_file "$scope") || exit 1
  [ -e "$file" ] || { echo "atlas-statusline: $file does not exist — nothing to remove."; exit 0; }
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
    echo "atlas-statusline: jq is not installed, so nothing was changed. Delete the \"statusLine\" key in $file." >&2
    exit 1
  }
  existing=$(jq -r '.statusLine.command // ""' "$file" 2>/dev/null)
  case $existing in
    '') echo "atlas-statusline: $file has no statusLine — nothing to remove."; exit 0 ;;
    atlas-statusline|*/atlas-statusline) ;;
    *)
      echo "atlas-statusline: the statusLine in $file is not this one, so it was left alone:"
      echo "  $existing"
      exit 1
      ;;
  esac
  tmp="$file.atlas.$$"
  if jq 'del(.statusLine)' "$file" > "$tmp" 2>/dev/null; then
    mv -- "$tmp" "$file"
    echo "atlas-statusline: removed from $file"
  else
    rm -f -- "$tmp"
    echo "atlas-statusline: could not write $file. Nothing was changed." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- arguments

scope=user
arg_dir=
for a in "$@"; do
  case $a in
    --project) scope=project ;;
    --install) action=install ;;
    --uninstall) action=uninstall ;;
    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
    -*) echo "atlas-statusline: unknown option $a" >&2; usage >&2; exit 1 ;;
    *) arg_dir=$a ;;
  esac
done

case ${action:-print} in
  install)   install_it "$scope"; exit 0 ;;
  uninstall) uninstall_it "$scope"; exit 0 ;;
esac

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the line itself

# **Only read stdin when something is piping into it.** `cat` on a terminal blocks forever, and the first
# thing anyone does with a statusline script is run it by hand to see what it prints. A tool that hangs when
# you try it is a tool nobody gets as far as installing.
payload=
[ -t 0 ] || payload=$(cat 2>/dev/null)

dir=$arg_dir
if [ -z "$dir" ] && [ -n "$payload" ]; then
  # The payload's `cwd` is authoritative: the directory this command is spawned in is not documented, and a
  # statusline that reads the wrong repository would report another project's port with total confidence —
  # which is the failure the derived-port scheme exists to prevent, not one to reintroduce here.
  dir=$(printf '%s' "$payload" | sed -n 's/.*"cwd"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' | head -1)
fi
# `$(pwd)` rather than `$PWD`, because `$PWD` is inherited: a parent that spawns this with a different
# working directory without updating the variable — which is what `child_process` does by default — leaves a
# stale value behind, and a stale value here means reporting another repository's port.
[ -n "$dir" ] || dir=$(pwd)
case $dir in /*) ;; *) dir=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$dir" 2>/dev/null && pwd) || exit 0 ;; esac

root=$(repo_root "$dir") || exit 0

# **Silence means one thing only: this repository has not adopted the tool.**
#
# Overloading it was the first version's mistake, and the config file is what separates the two cases. A
# repository with no `project-atlas.config.json` has no dashboard to have an opinion about, and printing a
# segment there would put atlas in the statusline of every unrelated project the user opens.
#
# A repository that *has* adopted it always gets a line, because from here on every silence would be a lie
# of omission. This is not hypothetical: the pidfile is the first casualty whenever servers race for a port
# — the record is keyed by a pid, so reaping a dead one throws away the claim while the winner keeps
# listening. Three of the four repositories on this machine reached exactly that state, still answering on
# their ports with no pidfile naming them, and a statusline that fell silent would have reported a working
# dashboard as an unadopted repository.
[ -f "$root/project-atlas.config.json" ] || exit 0

pidfile="$root/.atlas/serve.pid"
pid=$(pid_of "$pidfile" 2>/dev/null)
port=$(port_of "$pidfile" 2>/dev/null)

# A live pid is the only thing that earns a URL. Everything else — no pidfile, an unreadable one, a claim
# naming a process that has gone — is the same fact to a reader: adopted, and not currently reachable. It is
# also self-correcting, because the next tool use fires `on-activity.sh`, which brings the server back.
if [ -n "$pid" ] && [ -n "$port" ] && kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then
  printf 'atlas · http://127.0.0.1:%s/\n' "$port"
else
  printf 'atlas · dashboard down\n'
fi
exit 0
